    {
      "ordinal": 1,
      "source_number": "1085",
      "actual_task": "Given the natural solution (3,2) of (x+y*sqrt(2))(x-y*sqrt(2))=1, prove there are arbitrarily many other natural-number pairs.",
      "independent_solution": "The equation is x^2-2y^2=1. Starting with (x1,y1)=(3,2), define x(n+1)=3*xn+4*yn and y(n+1)=2*xn+3*yn. Integer positivity is preserved. Direct expansion gives (3x+4y)^2-2(2x+3y)^2=(9-8)x^2+(24-24)xy+(16-18)y^2=x^2-2y^2. Thus every constructed pair satisfies the original equation. Since x(n+1)-xn=2*xn+4*yn>0, the pairs are all distinct. For any positive integer m, the pairs with n=2,...,m+1 give m other solutions, excluding the supplied pair.",
      "concrete_construction": {
        "first_pairs": [
          "(3,2)",
          "(17,12)",
          "(99,70)"
        ],
        "checks": [
          "3^2-2*2^2=9-8=1",
          "17^2-2*12^2=289-288=1",
          "99^2-2*70^2=9801-9800=1"
        ],
        "equivalent_expression": "xn+yn*sqrt(2)=(3+2*sqrt(2))^n for n>=1; multiplication by the conjugate gives norm1."
      },
      "qualifications": [
        "Every constructed x and y is strictly positive, so the proof is valid whether or not a natural-number convention includes0.",
        "The source requests arbitrarily many other pairs, not classification of every Pell-equation solution. This unbounded distinct family is sufficient; no completeness classification is added as a source requirement.",
        "The construction preserves the original ordered roles of x and y."
      ],
      "source_status": "proved; no unresolved premise needed for this construction"
    },
    {
      "ordinal": 2,
      "source_number": "1087",
      "actual_task": "For x^2+p*x+1=0, the sum of the squares of its roots is254; determine coefficient P.",
      "independent_solution": "Let the two roots, counted with multiplicity, be alpha and beta. Vieta gives alpha+beta=-p and alpha*beta=1. Therefore alpha^2+beta^2=(alpha+beta)^2-2*alpha*beta=p^2-2=254, so p^2=256 and p=16 or p=-16. Both values satisfy the condition; no sign condition in the source removes either.",
      "root_and_sufficiency_checks": [
        "For p=16, x=-8+3*sqrt(7) or x=-8-3*sqrt(7). Their product is64-63=1 and square sum is2*(64+63)=254.",
        "For p=-16, x=8+3*sqrt(7) or x=8-3*sqrt(7). Their product is64-63=1 and square sum is254.",
        "For either coefficient, discriminant p^2-4=252=36*7>0, so the roots are real and distinct."
      ],
      "complete_requested_answer": [
        "p=16",
        "p=-16"
      ],
      "qualifications": [
        "The equation uses lowercase p and the final command uppercase P. I interpret P as the same coefficient being requested, while recording the printed case mismatch. If P were a separate symbol, the source supplies no relation determining it.",
        "The source says squares, not squared absolute values. Ordinary algebraic root squares give these same two coefficients whether one initially allows real or complex roots; both resulting root pairs are real.",
        "No positive-root or positive-coefficient restriction is stated and none is imposed."
      ],
      "source_status": "two coefficient solutions, subject only to the recorded p/P typographic identification"
    },
    {
      "ordinal": 3,
      "source_number": "1089",
      "actual_task": "Prove that a square has maximum area among all rectangles of a fixed perimeter.",
      "independent_solution": "Let the fixed perimeter be L>0 and the side lengths a,b>0. Then a+b=L/2. Since (a-b)^2>=0, 4ab<=(a+b)^2=L^2/4, and area ab<=L^2/16. More precisely L^2/16-ab=(a-b)^2/4. Equality holds exactly when a=b=L/4, which is an allowed square with the prescribed perimeter. It attains the bound, so it is the maximum rather than merely a supremum.",
      "maximum": "L^2/16, attained exactly at the square of side L/4",
      "qualifications": [
        "A nondegenerate rectangle has positive side lengths and positive perimeter. If degenerate rectangles with nonnegative sides are also allowed at L>0, their areas are0 and they do not change the maximum.",
        "L=0 gives no nondegenerate rectangle; a negative perimeter is not a geometric input.",
        "The equality criterion is part of proving the asserted square maximizer, not an additional source task inferred from a prior solver."
      ],
      "source_status": "proved for the ordinary geometric domain"
    },
    {
      "ordinal": 4,
      "source_number": "1090",
      "printed_number_in_seed_text": "1030",
      "actual_task": "Prove that the remainder when a prime is divided by30 is a prime or1.",
      "independent_solution": "Use ordinary nonnegative integer division p=30q+r with0<=r<30. The primes2,3,5 have remainders2,3,5 themselves. For any prime p>5, none of2,3,5 divides p, so none divides r. The residues in0,...,29 coprime to30 are1,7,11,13,17,19,23,29. All except1 are prime. Hence the claimed conclusion holds for every prime, including those below30. Equivalently, a composite remainder below30 would have a prime factor no greater than sqrt(29)<6, thus2,3 or5; that factor would divide p, contradicting primality unless p itself were2,3 or5, which already have prime remainders.",
      "complete_possible_remainder_set": [
