    {
      "ordinal": 5,
      "source_number": "1091",
      "actual_task": "A motorboat takes8 hours downstream from A to B and10 hours upstream back to A. Determine the raft's A-to-B travel time.",
      "independent_solution": "Under the standard uniform-current model, let distance be d>0, still-water boat speed v and current speed u>0, with v>u. The two observations give v+u=d/8 and v-u=d/10. Subtracting yields2u=d*(1/8-1/10)=d/40, hence u=d/80. An unpowered raft moves with the current, so its time is d/u=80 hours. Adding the equations gives v=9d/80=9u, which is consistent with the required upstream motion.",
      "conditional_answer": "80 hours",
      "qualifications": [
        "The calculation uses the same still-water boat speed on both trips, a steady current over the route, the same A-B distance, and travel times without added waiting. A passive raft is advected at the current speed. These are the conventional motion-model meanings, not explicitly detailed physical measurements in the source.",
        "If the boat's still-water speeds may differ between directions, the data alone do not identify u: for any0<u<d/8, choose v_down=d/8-u and v_up=d/10+u. Both observed times then hold while the raft time d/u varies. Thus80 is not an unconditional theorem for arbitrary changing propulsion or current.",
        "No specific distance, equal-density assumption or new numerical premise is invented."
      ],
      "source_status": "80 hours under the recorded standard boat/current model; nonuniform variants are underdetermined"
    },
    {
      "ordinal": 6,
      "source_number": "1092",
      "actual_task": "Mix zinc/copper alloys in ratios1:2 and2:3 to obtain ratio17:27; determine how many parts of each alloy to use.",
      "independent_solution": "First use positive amounts a,b of the two complete alloys in one common additive mass unit, and interpret each stated metal ratio as zinc:copper by mass. Alloy1 has zinc fraction1/3 and copper fraction2/3; alloy2 has fractions2/5 and3/5. The target requires (a/3+2b/5)/(2a/3+3b/5)=17/27. The denominator is positive, so cross-multiplication is legitimate. Multiplying by15 gives27*(5a+6b)=17*(10a+9b), that is135a+162b=170a+153b, hence9b=35a. Thus a:b=9:35. Conversely a=9t,b=35t with t>0 gives zinc3t+14t=17t and copper6t+21t=27t, exactly the target ratio.",
      "complete_common_mass_amount_family": "a=9t, b=35t for any t>0",
      "equal_mass_part_count_conclusion": "If each part means the same mass unit for either complete alloy, minimal positive integer counts are9 and35; every integer-count pair is(9k,35k), k a positive integer. The source states neither a total mass nor a minimality requirement, so absolute quantities are not unique.",
      "different_recipe_batch_unit_conclusion": "If one first-alloy part means a whole recipe batch containing1 unit zinc and2 units copper (total3 units), and one second-alloy part means a batch containing2 units zinc and3 units copper (total5 units), let their counts be u,v. The mixture equation is(u+2v)/(2u+3v)=17/27, giving27u+54v=34u+51v, so3v=7u and u:v=3:7. Minimal such recipe-batch counts are3 and7. Their actual alloy masses are3u:5v=9:35, agreeing with the mass result rather than contradicting it.",
      "qualifications": [
        "The supplied prose does not explicitly define the size or measure of a part, whether the metal ratios are by mass or another conserved additive measure, or whether counts must be integer/minimal. Exact original parts-unit intent remains UNKNOWN/HOLD.",
        "9:35 is the relative amount ratio for equal units of the complete alloys in the common mass model.3:7 counts unequal-size recipe batches. Neither raw count pair is silently chosen as the unqualified original answer.",
        "If the alloy composition ratios are by mass but parts refer to volumes, densities are needed: with densities rho1,rho2, the required volume ratio is V1:V2=9*rho2:35*rho1. No equal-density or volume-additivity premise is supplied or invented.",
        "For any specified conserved additive measure consistently used for both compositions and alloy amounts, the same fraction algebra yields9:35 in that measure. Converting between different measures or different-sized units needs additional definitions.",
        "The target zinc fraction17/44 lies strictly between1/3 and2/5, so positive mixing amounts are possible and neither component can be zero. No chemical loss or unmeasured added metal is assumed."
