    {
      "ordinal": 7,
      "source_number": "1093",
      "actual_task": "Two motorcyclists start simultaneously from opposite ends, first meet50 km from B, continue to the opposite endpoints and immediately turn, then meet25 km from A. Determine A-B distance.",
      "independent_solution": "Under constant individual speeds on the same segment, let its length be L>50 km. Denote speeds by vA,vB>0 according to starting point. At the first meeting they have traveled L-50 and50 in the same time, so vA/vB=(L-50)/50. At the specified return-leg meeting25 km from A, the A-starter has traveled L+(L-25)=2L-25, and the B-starter L+25. They again have equal total elapsed time, hence(2L-25)/(L+25)=(L-50)/50. Cross-multiplying:100L-1250=L^2-25L-1250, so L^2-125L=0. The roots are0 and125; L>50 discards0. Thus the distance is125 km.",
      "timeline_and_sufficiency": "For L=125 the speed ratio is75:50=3:2. Write vA=3s, vB=2s with s>0. The first meeting time is25/s, at position75 km from A, hence50 km from B. Endpoint arrival times are125/(3s) and125/(2s), both earlier than the return meeting75/s. At that meeting the A-starter has covered225 km and the B-starter150 km, placing both25 km from A. Before the B-starter's turn, the faster returning A-starter cannot catch the still-outbound B-starter: their predicted catch would be at125/s, later than the B-starter's turn. Thus the described meeting chronology is consistent.",
      "conditional_answer": "125 km",
      "qualifications": [
        "The usual model has each rider preserve their own positive speed, use the same A-B route, and reverse immediately at the opposite endpoint. The two speeds need not be equal.",
        "Without constant individual speeds or with unreported stops/route changes, the two meeting positions do not alone determine a unique distance.",
        "The source names endpoint arrivals collectively as A and B; the calculation treats each rider as continuing to the opposite endpoint before turning, not as already returning to their own starting point before the stated turn.",
        "The zero root is an algebraic extraneous candidate excluded by the first interior meeting and positive distance, not a second physical answer."
      ],
      "source_status": "unique125 km in the recorded standard motion model"
    },
