      "ordinal": 8,
      "source_number": "1094",
      "actual_task": "A traveler walks41 km from M to N at5 km/h. A rider starts2 hours later, overtakes the traveler, reaches N, immediately returns, and meets the traveler2 hours45 minutes after overtaking. Determine rider speed.",
      "independent_solution": "Let the rider have one constant speed v km/h in both directions. Measure t in hours from the traveler's departure. Before the turn, traveler position is5t and rider position v*(t-2). To overtake requires v>5, and the first meeting time is t1=2v/(v-5), with position x1=10v/(v-5). The elapsed time after that overtake is Delta=2+45/60=11/4 hours, not the time after rider departure. At the return meeting the traveler is at x2=x1+5*Delta=x1+55/4. The rider's distance between meetings is(41-x1)+(41-x2)=82-2x1-55/4. Therefore(11/4)*v=273/4-2x1, or11v=273-80v/(v-5). Multiplication by v-5>0 gives11v^2-248v+1365=0=(v-13)*(11v-105). Thus the only algebraic candidates in this model are v=13 and v=105/11. Both satisfy all original chronological requirements, as checked below, so both must be retained.",
